  ":question/id": "cecd5cf6-ba72-50d3-91d2-17f1da2ed445",
  ":question/math-academy-id": "q-317026",
  ":question/problem": "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}2x-3y \\\\ -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$T=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>",
  ":question/worked-solution": "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot y \\\\ (-4)\\cdot x+0\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-3y \\\\ -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"
}
